# Negative case 3: a container truncated to 300 bytes.
# vendor/streamdb-v3/negative/corpus.trunc300.streamdb is `head -c 300
# corpus.streamdb`, so BOTH header slots survive intact -- magic, version and
# each slot's own trailing CRC all still validate -- and both slots'
# trie/index offsets and lengths point past the end of the 300-byte file.
#
# What expectation.json records the reference reader doing: header_parse's
# bounds check fails for both slots, read_commit finds no valid commit, and
# probe and open both return STREAMDB_EMB_ERR_FORMAT ("bad format").
#
# What this reader does: `caput_elige` answers 256 (no commit) and
# `probatio.exsc` returns 1 having written NOTHING. There is no `stdout=` key,
# so tests/run.sh requires stdout to be empty -- a reader that emitted a
# partial stream before noticing would fail here rather than pass quietly.
#
# THIS IS THE ONE CASE THE BOUNDS CHECK DECIDES, and it decides it on offsets
# a real writer wrote. It does NOT exercise the non-wrapping FORM of that
# check: `off + len > file_len` and `len > file_len || off > file_len - len`
# agree on every header in this corpus. Mutant 4 in the commit message is
# where the form is proved, on a header made by hand, because the corpus
# cannot make one.
TEST: expect-exit=1 stdin=vendor/streamdb-v3/negative/corpus.trunc300.streamdb sources=examples/streamdb/lector_streamdb.exsc,examples/streamdb/probatio.exsc
